WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.740
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:13.380
Today we are going to clone real human voices&nbsp;
using an AI. How? Well, in an earlier NVIDIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:20.520
keynote, we had a look at Jensen Jr., an&nbsp;
AI-powered virtual assistant of NVIDIA’s CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:32.520
Jensen Huang. It could do, this! Look&nbsp;
at the face of that proud man! I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.860
how it also uses hand gestures that&nbsp;
go really well with the explanation.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:44.460
These virtual AI assistants are going to appear&nbsp;
everywhere to help you with your daily tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.680
For instance, in your car, the promise&nbsp;
is that they will be able to recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:56.160
you as the owner of the car, recommend&nbsp;
shows nearby, and even drive you there.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:03.720
These Omniverse avatars may also help us order our&nbsp;
favorite burgers too. And, we won’t even need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.440
push buttons on a touchscreen. We just need to&nbsp;
say what we wish to eat and the assistant will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:17.760
answer and take our orders, perhaps later even&nbsp;
in a familiar person’s voice. How cool is that?

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.240
And today, I am going to ask you to imagine a&nbsp;
future where we can all have our Toy Jensens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:32.220
or our own virtual assistants with our own&nbsp;
voice. All of us! That sounds really cool! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:39.840
is that in the far future? No-no, not at all!&nbsp;
Today, I have the amazing opportunity to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.720
you a bit more about the AI that makes&nbsp;
this voice synthesis happen. And yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.520
you will hear things that are only&nbsp;
available here at Two Minute Papers.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:57.540
So, what is all this about? This work is an&nbsp;
AI-based technique that takes samples of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:04.980
voice, and can then clone it. So, let’s give it a&nbsp;
try. This is Jamil from NVIDIA who was kind enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:17.580
to record these voice snippets. Listen. Okay, so&nbsp;
how much of this do we need to train the AI? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:25.260
not the entire life recordings of the test&nbsp;
subject, but much less, 30 minutes of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:31.380
voice samples. The technique asks us to say&nbsp;
these sentences and analyzes the timbre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:39.000
prosody and the rhythm of our voice, which is&nbsp;
quite a task. And, what can it do afterwards?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.420
Well, hold on to your papers, because Jamil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:49.200
not the real one, the cloned AI Jamil has&nbsp;
a scholarly message for you that I wrote.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:25.740
Now, of course, it is not perfect, I think&nbsp;
that most of you are able to tell that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:32.220
voice lines were synthesized, but my opinion is&nbsp;
that these are easily good enough for a helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:40.200
humanlike virtual assistant. And, really, how cool&nbsp;
is that? Cloning a human voice from half an hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:47.040
worth of sound samples. What a time to be alive!&nbsp;
And note that I have been really tough with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:53.280
these are some long, scholarly sentences that&nbsp;
would give a challenge to any of these algorithms.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.820
Now, if you are one of our earlier Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars, you might remember that a few years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:06.360
we talked about an AI technique called Tacotron,&nbsp;
which can perform voice cloning from a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:12.240
short, few second-long sample. I have only heard&nbsp;
examples of simpler, shorter sentences for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.940
and what is new here is that this new&nbsp;
technique takes more data, but in return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:26.100
offers higher quality. But it does not stop there!&nbsp;
It does more! This new method is easier to train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:33.360
and it also generalizes to more languages better.&nbsp;
And these are already good enough to be used in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:39.840
real products, so I wonder what a technique like&nbsp;
this will look like just two more papers down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:47.580
line. Maybe my voice here on Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
could be synthesized by an AI? Maybe it already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:54.960
is? Would that be a good thing? What do you think?&nbsp;
Also, a virtual Károly for you to read your daily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:03.060
dose of papers? Actually, since NVIDIA has a great&nbsp;
track record of putting these amazing tools into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:09.240
everyone’s hands, if you are interested, there&nbsp;
is already an early access program where you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.540
apply. I hope that some of you will be able to&nbsp;
try this and let us know in the comments below!

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.620
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

